WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:08.690
Okay, Diocora.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:12.530
So I do want to play a Jebaba London game.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.810
Unfortunately, yesterday, or the last

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.290
speedrun game where I tried to do that,

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.770
my opponent played g6, so we ended up

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.610
transposing to a modern with e4 here.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.550
Hopefully we get an opportunity to play

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.850
the Jebaba, and we will.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:27.170
And we will.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:29.070
We play the Jebaba London.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.170
Hopefully I don't make a fool of myself.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.630
a6 is one of the more common moves, I

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:37.530
would say, along with e6 and Bf5.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.350
It's like the main trifecta.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.730
I almost put g6 in a separate category

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:42.990
because it's a completely different

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.110
type of position that you get.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.410
The lines a6, e6, Bf5, and to a certain

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.070
extent c6 all lead to pretty similar

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:54.010
positions, which the kind of

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.050
commonality is that the Jebaba London

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:57.430
appears very innocuous.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.750
It doesn't appear all that dangerous.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.010
It doesn't seem like an attacking

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.110
opening, but all of that is an illusion.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.710
It's actually a very, very aggressive

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.990
system, and a lot of people get in

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.810
trouble because they don't realize how

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.450
vast White's attacking potential is.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.630
So we respond with e3.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:14.870
This is a move that you always make in

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:16.530
the Jebaba, and c5 is already a

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.450
mistake, right?

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.210
Another example of how tricky the

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.790
Jebaba London is, c5 is an attempt to

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.430
punish us for developing our knight to

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.470
c3, because in the London system, the

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.390
traditional London, when you face the

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:32.050
move c5, you typically respond with c3.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.210
You build the pawn triangle so that d4

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:35.690
is well defended, but the great thing

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.690
about putting the knight on c3 is that

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.290
you're exerting immediate pressure on

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:40.110
the d5 pawn.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.030
That means that there is a move to play

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.790
here that you don't often play in the

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.670
traditional London, and the reason this

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.210
move makes sense here is because after

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:50.970
this move, the pressure on d5

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.130
intensifies, and it makes it harder for

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.670
Black to win back the pawn.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.210
Of course, I'm implying that the move

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.030
is d take c5, and it is surprisingly

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.410
problematic for Black to win back this

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.670
pawn without falling behind and developing.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.510
Knight c6 is the most common response,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:13.250
and you may think that the automatic

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.330
response to that is knight to f3.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.050
This is where getting the course yields

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:18.870
a lot of benefits.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.210
Knight f3 is not the correct move here,

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.430
even though it's by far the most common

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.190
move online.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.850
The correct move is a3.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.290
Now, the idea of a3 should be very

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:30.130
obvious to everybody.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.910
We want to play b4 and secure the pawn

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.170
on c5, but the flaw inherent in this

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.490
move seems to be that Black plays e5,

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.570
grabs control of the center, and then

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:42.150
wins the pawn on c5 with interest, but

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:42.950
there is a catch.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.730
Rather than meekly dropping back to g3,

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.430
we put the bishop on g5, and now the

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.150
power of our knight development really

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:51.870
shines through.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:53.670
We're threatening the very simple

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.330
bishop takes f6 and knight takes d5,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.330
which would knock out a key central

00:02:58.330 --> 00:02:59.890
pawn and retain our material advantage.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.310
Black has to waste the tempo playing

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.070
bishop e6.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.130
What does this give us the opportunity

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:05.750
to do?

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.550
Well, this gives us the opportunity now

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:12.230
to secure the c5 pawn with a move b4,

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.430
and that's why we played a3.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.150
White is up a pawn for no compensation.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.710
Okay, now we're exiting the opening

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.010
stage, and we're entering the middle

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.410
game stage, and we have to be accurate.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.050
So because we spent a couple of tempi

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:29.030
crafting our pawn structure on the

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:30.610
queen side, we have fallen behind in

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:31.930
development a little bit, so we need to

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:32.490
remedy that.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.230
So the obvious move would be knight to f3.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.490
Another typical idea in these lines is

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:41.250
to transfer the knight from c3 to b6.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.650
So knight a4 is a move that you see

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.510
very often in this line.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:46.690
I don't see the rush.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.350
I don't see the need to do that immediately.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:49.970
We'll have to check the course

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:51.370
afterward to make sure I'm getting the

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.050
move order right, but I'm pretty sure

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:54.370
that the move is knight to f3.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.910
Black typically castles, and we

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.930
continue our development not with

00:03:58.930 --> 00:03:59.770
bishop to d3.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.950
That walks right into the fork, but the

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.890
bishop is developed more modestly to e2.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:05.310
That's it.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.350
We're up a full pawn, and more

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.830
importantly, it's not just any pawn.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:10.530
This pawn makes it difficult for Black

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:11.510
to breathe, right?

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.930
It's just controlling squares that

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:16.190
Black would like to use to get his

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:17.670
pieces into the game more efficiently.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:19.730
A lot of people go queen d7 in a

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:20.570
position like this.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.870
Then the effectiveness of knight a4 is increased.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:26.250
Hopefully you see why, because once the

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.170
knight hits b6, that's actually a fork.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.010
At the very least, it'll come with tempo.

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:33.670
So I would say that the compensation is

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.230
very limited, because just having more

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.830
pawns in the center does not yield compensation.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.570
There's a question in the chat from

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:41.250
Peculiar Estate.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.230
Would you really say there's no compensation?

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:44.810
Black is better center and fairly

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:45.710
classical development.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.650
So we also have classical development, right?

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.410
The pieces are actually developed very similarly.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:52.210
If you compare the placement of the

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.610
knights, the placement of these two

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:56.470
bishops, basically development is symmetrical.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:58.210
The only big difference is that Black

00:04:58.210 --> 00:04:59.130
has more pawns in the center.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.850
But both of these pawns are also liabilities.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:03.990
We're attacking and pressuring both

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:04.870
pawns with our knights.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.070
Our pawn on e3 is doing the job.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.130
It's restricting the mobility of

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:09.550
Black's pawns.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.930
It's making the move d4 a lot less

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.770
dangerous than it otherwise would be.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:15.610
It's like a speed bump, right?

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:17.510
That's not really allowing the card to accelerate.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:21.050
If Black plays d4, then we would likely

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:22.750
snap off the pawn.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.950
We would trade once, and then we would

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.370
garner the e4 square for our knight by

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.570
first taking on f6, then sticking our

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:31.750
knight in the center on e4.

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:33.350
Queen e8.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:36.050
I mean, that is a tricky move.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:39.290
It sets a trap that I'm seeing through already.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.530
The trap that it sets is if bishop

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:43.770
takes f6, bishop takes f6, knight takes

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.390
d5, what you should notice is the

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:48.170
alignment of Black's bishop and white's

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:48.810
rook in the corner.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.310
So Black has this move, e4, but not

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:52.690
immediately, because immediately we

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.310
would take the bishop on f6.

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:57.130
The problem is Black first takes on d5,

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:58.470
then plays e4.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.490
That's a discovered attack against the knight.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:02.810
We do have knight d4 in that position,

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.250
but if you keep calculating, Black can

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.610
take on d4 and then finally play rook

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:09.570
d8 and win the d4 pawn back with interest.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:12.930
I'm not interested in these kinds of

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.430
tactics because we're simply up a pawn.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.830
Let's proceed simply, and I will show

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:19.950
this over the board after the game, so

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:20.370
don't worry.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.030
Okay, there's no reason for us not to castle.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:24.930
Clearly our opponent wants to put a

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:27.150
rook on d8 and x -ray our queen and

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:28.390
maybe prepare the move d4.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:29.630
No, knight e4.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:31.310
Okay, did I blunder?

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:32.110
I might have blundered.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:32.490
Whoa.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:34.350
Whoa.

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:35.910
What is knight e4?

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:37.030
I did blunder this move.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:37.870
Oh my gosh.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:44.420
Wow, queen e8 is unbelievable, and the

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:45.220
idea is knight e4.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:45.880
I didn't see that.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.580
I didn't see that at all.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.680
That is not in the course, needless to say.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:54.340
Knight takes e4, de, bishop e7.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:55.760
The idea is ef.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:56.680
I mean, we're not worse.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:58.000
I think position is equal.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.500
Position is equal, but that's brilliant

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.100
by our opponent, and I'm learning new

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:03.660
things about the line.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:04.740
So basically, what did I miss?

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.960
If the queen was still on d8, then we

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.320
would be able to take the bishop on e7,

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.580
and we would be attacking the queen, so

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:14.080
Black would have to spend that tempo

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:15.160
recapturing the bishop.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:17.720
Now we can't do that, so while I still

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.120
have to take on e7, White, Black now

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:21.080
takes on f3.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:22.800
I have to take the rook.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:23.440
Black takes.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.000
We take, and at the end of the day, we

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.240
get two pawns and a rook for two minor

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.320
pieces, so we're still—I would probably

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:32.060
prefer White here, but definitely, I

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:32.760
should have seen that.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:34.440
Still, I think White is doing

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:35.080
completely fine.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:36.900
Now, when you have a rook against two

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:38.100
pieces, we haven't had too many of

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:39.360
these games in the speedrun, so I think

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:39.980
it's a good thing.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.540
The evaluation of a rook for two pieces

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.800
type situations depends primarily on

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.120
the relative activity of the rook

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.140
versus the minor pieces.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.480
So if you're the side of the rook or

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.640
the two rooks, you have to aim for

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:54.680
total rook activity, ASAP.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.320
So in this case, the move is a no -brainer.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:57.960
We need to put something on d1.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:07:59.080
That's the only open file.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.160
I propose that we put the f -rook on d1

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.840
so that the rook on a1 kind of

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.680
supervised some of these queenside pawns.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.320
Like if Black plays a5 or something,

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:11.920
it's a good idea, I think, to keep one

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.140
of the rooks on the queenside for the

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:13.580
time being.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:17.560
I'm also eyeing the juicy d6 square for

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:18.040
our rook.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:20.520
We can stick a rook on d6, and we can

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.900
use that as an anchor point and then

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:24.000
double rooks on the d -file.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:25.760
Still, I think White is doing

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:26.840
completely fine here.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:28.660
After the game, we'll check exactly

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:29.340
where I went wrong.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.140
I mean, castles is kind of a mini

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:34.420
blunder, but I'm just stunned by this

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:35.980
idea, Qe8 and Nd4.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:37.420
Incredible.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.660
So still, I think White is doing fine here.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.520
Yeah, rook d6 is ultimately the idea.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:45.920
Suddenly, our opponent is finally

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.220
thinking, which has been pretty rare

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:49.680
thus far.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:51.200
I actually think Black's position is

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.100
hard to play, partially because the

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.660
knight on c6 is fully restricted.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.540
We're controlling d4, we're controlling b4.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:00.580
The bishop on e6 is fine, but it has no

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:01.760
jumping off points.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:05.060
Yeah, it's not as much of a blunder as

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:05.360
I thought.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.840
It was sort of a visceral reaction.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.160
I thought it was a blunder, but now

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:09.420
when I look at this position, I like

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:09.920
White a lot.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:12.180
I think if Black handles this well, it

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.040
should be closer to equal.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.040
The rook trade is probably good for us.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:18.880
As a general rule of thumb, and this is

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.200
very general, if there's a situation

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.540
two rooks versus two minor pieces in a

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:26.180
rook, a trade of a pair of rooks favors

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:27.180
the side with the rook.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:28.960
A lot of situations where it's pure

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:31.020
rook versus two minor pieces, and there

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.220
are no other pieces on the board, the

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.460
rook often dominates simply because the

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:37.060
rook has long range ability, and the

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.660
bishop and the knight's long range

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:41.440
ability to ferry it from side to side

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:42.680
is restricted.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.140
The rook often trampolines from one

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.080
side of the board to the other and wins

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:48.920
a bunch of pawns because the minor

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.700
pieces are incompetent at defending weaknesses.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:53.520
I think this is an excellent move that

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.380
our opponent just played because this

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.160
opens up a square for the knight.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.460
Yeah, this is not going to be an easy game.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:00.520
I can already sense that.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.120
Oh wow, rook d6, knight e5, rook d1,

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:04.420
there's bishop g4, okay.

