00:41:23.090 --> 00:41:24.630
In this case, it's to force the knight

00:41:24.630 --> 00:41:26.550
off of e5, also to create the

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:27.570
possibility of a rook trade.

00:41:28.070 --> 00:41:29.990
Probably a b was another mistake.

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:31.230
I didn't understand why our opponent

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:31.690
did that.

00:41:31.770 --> 00:41:34.750
It undoubles our pawns, and after 95,

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:37.370
we still had the move rook to d8,

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:39.810
perhaps, but I think this move makes a

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:40.590
lot of sense here.

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:42.750
Queen c5, queen b7, and white is

00:41:42.750 --> 00:41:44.010
playing completely without risk,

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:46.750
because even if white loses the b5

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:48.170
pawn, it's still a draw.

00:41:48.530 --> 00:41:51.230
Four on three, and then a rook against

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:53.050
two minor pieces is typically a draw.

00:41:53.130 --> 00:41:55.010
So at this point, I want to show two

00:41:55.010 --> 00:41:57.710
examples of the battle of rook and

00:41:57.710 --> 00:42:00.210
queen versus minor pieces to really

00:42:00.210 --> 00:42:01.570
increase your understanding,

00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:03.150
particularly of playing with the heavy

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:04.190
pieces, the rook or the queen.

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:05.850
So here's one of my favorite games.

00:42:05.910 --> 00:42:07.490
This is not very well known, but it

00:42:07.490 --> 00:42:08.950
features a really, really classic

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:09.690
endgame moment.

00:42:10.490 --> 00:42:12.410
I'm going to pull up the game in just a moment.

00:42:12.490 --> 00:42:13.710
I have to find it on chess space.

00:42:14.610 --> 00:42:16.470
This is the game, Mikhalevsky -Kagansky.

00:42:16.550 --> 00:42:19.850
Mikhalevsky, very, very strong GM from Israel.

00:42:19.910 --> 00:42:23.750
He is also known as a noted chess coach.

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:25.890
He's written opening books, so

00:42:25.890 --> 00:42:28.590
definitely a name to revere.

00:42:28.910 --> 00:42:31.910
Here he has white against, you know, 2300FM.

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:36.150
Okay, so in this position, it is white

00:42:36.150 --> 00:42:37.090
to play, okay?

00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:40.750
Okay, so what's going on?

00:42:40.790 --> 00:42:42.510
Well, it's clearly two rooks against

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:43.650
two minor pieces and a rook.

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:46.390
First thing that white does is follow

00:42:46.390 --> 00:42:46.930
the rules.

00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:49.570
White has a very easy clap way to force

00:42:49.570 --> 00:42:51.210
the rook trade, and by forcing the rook

00:42:51.210 --> 00:42:53.290
trade, white is isolating the minor pieces.

00:42:53.430 --> 00:42:54.130
So what does white play?

00:42:54.550 --> 00:42:54.890
Oops, sorry.

00:42:54.970 --> 00:42:57.270
Mikhalevsky plays rook to c3, rook to c3.

00:42:58.090 --> 00:42:59.590
This attacks the bishop, forces the

00:42:59.590 --> 00:43:00.790
bishop to move, and now he trades.

00:43:01.450 --> 00:43:02.550
So step one has been achieved.

00:43:02.710 --> 00:43:05.190
Step two is to make sure that black is

00:43:05.190 --> 00:43:06.930
as paralyzed as possible.

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:08.850
So now he brings the other rook to the

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:10.550
8th rank in order to paralyze the knight.

00:43:11.350 --> 00:43:12.230
Bishop has to move.

00:43:12.370 --> 00:43:15.030
Rook goes back to the 7th rank, and the

00:43:15.030 --> 00:43:15.910
bishop moves back to 8th.

00:43:15.930 --> 00:43:17.430
Essentially an implicit draw for white

00:43:17.430 --> 00:43:19.070
can repeat moves now with rook c8 and

00:43:19.070 --> 00:43:19.550
rook c7.

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:21.650
Now there's a move here that I think 90

00:43:21.650 --> 00:43:23.470
% of people would play, and then there's

00:43:23.470 --> 00:43:24.750
the move Mikhalevsky played, which is

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:26.330
absolutely freaking brilliant.

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:29.310
Okay, obviously black's pieces are very passive.

00:43:29.410 --> 00:43:31.030
The move that 90 % of people would play,

00:43:31.110 --> 00:43:32.370
probably you're thinking about this

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:35.030
move on YouTube, is rook a7 because,

00:43:35.270 --> 00:43:37.550
you know, we see pawns and we take them.

00:43:37.670 --> 00:43:39.210
Bishop c6 and rook takes a5.

00:43:39.590 --> 00:43:42.050
The downside of this approach is that

00:43:42.050 --> 00:43:44.310
it finally opens up a pathway for black

00:43:44.310 --> 00:43:45.670
to get his pieces into the game.

00:43:46.130 --> 00:43:47.850
So something like knight e6 is now

00:43:47.850 --> 00:43:49.090
possible because the knight is no

00:43:49.090 --> 00:43:49.610
longer pinned.

00:43:50.470 --> 00:43:52.690
Now of course black also attacks d4.

00:43:53.130 --> 00:43:55.570
Probably white would now play...

00:43:55.570 --> 00:43:57.250
I don't actually know what white does now.

00:43:57.370 --> 00:43:58.890
I mean white could play b3 to try to

00:43:58.890 --> 00:44:00.970
create a pass pawn on the queen side,

00:44:00.990 --> 00:44:02.510
but here black plays knight takes d4,

00:44:02.590 --> 00:44:04.410
and clearly you've let black off the hook.

00:44:04.810 --> 00:44:06.010
I mean you're not even close to

00:44:06.010 --> 00:44:07.910
creating a passer, and black is going

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:09.330
to start bringing his king into the center.

00:44:10.390 --> 00:44:13.710
So instead, Mikhalevsky finds a

00:44:13.710 --> 00:44:14.250
brilliant move.

00:44:14.330 --> 00:44:16.110
Some of you are suggesting rook c5.

00:44:16.230 --> 00:44:17.330
It's the same idea.

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:18.470
This move has the same...

00:44:18.470 --> 00:44:19.010
I agree.

00:44:19.190 --> 00:44:21.490
Rook c5 is better than rook a7 because

00:44:21.490 --> 00:44:23.790
here you win the b -pawn, and by winning

00:44:23.790 --> 00:44:25.750
the b -pawn you leave black with a

00:44:25.750 --> 00:44:26.810
really passive a -pawn.

00:44:26.990 --> 00:44:30.530
True, but still black will play, for

00:44:30.530 --> 00:44:34.370
instance, let me think, maybe f6, okay,

00:44:34.390 --> 00:44:35.830
to open up a pathway for the king.

00:44:36.270 --> 00:44:38.410
You will take on f6 and take on b5, and

00:44:38.410 --> 00:44:39.930
white is probably winning here.

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:41.830
I agree, but again you've let the minor

00:44:41.830 --> 00:44:44.410
pieces get out of their cage, and black

00:44:44.410 --> 00:44:46.410
has chances because the minor pieces

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:46.890
get active.

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:48.910
So instead, here's what Mikhalevsky

00:44:48.910 --> 00:44:51.610
does, and here's how he arrives at that logic.

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:54.210
Okay, what is black's next move going

00:44:54.210 --> 00:44:54.550
to be?

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:57.210
If you gave black the move, how is

00:44:57.210 --> 00:44:58.630
black going to untangle?

00:44:59.330 --> 00:45:00.470
Well you might say, well black is going

00:45:00.470 --> 00:45:01.710
to move the knight, but no, black's not

00:45:01.710 --> 00:45:03.070
going to do that because this is a fork.

00:45:03.290 --> 00:45:04.950
So in order to be able to move the

00:45:04.950 --> 00:45:06.330
knight, black has to get his king off

00:45:06.330 --> 00:45:08.090
the eighth rank so that rook c8

00:45:08.090 --> 00:45:12.430
wouldn't be a So black's next move is

00:45:12.430 --> 00:45:14.850
g6, and that's the only way to untangle.

00:45:15.730 --> 00:45:17.590
Now we skip a move, black then plays

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:20.330
king g7, and only then black is ready

00:45:20.330 --> 00:45:21.810
to bring the knight out to e6 because

00:45:21.810 --> 00:45:24.250
now rook c8 is into check, and the

00:45:24.250 --> 00:45:26.150
bishop is able to wiggle out.

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:28.490
So Mikhalevsky finds an idea that

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:30.730
prevents black from ever untangling.

00:45:30.850 --> 00:45:33.950
He plays g6 himself and puts the pawn

00:45:33.950 --> 00:45:36.430
on pre to three different defenders.

00:45:36.630 --> 00:45:38.050
Knight g6, rook c8, we know.

00:45:38.410 --> 00:45:39.390
So black takes with the pawn.

00:45:39.910 --> 00:45:41.890
Now the second step, you have literally

00:45:41.890 --> 00:45:45.930
put a weight on the g6 square, but now

00:45:45.930 --> 00:45:47.870
you need to prevent black from going g5

00:45:47.870 --> 00:45:48.810
and g6.

00:45:49.210 --> 00:45:54.050
White plays f4, stopping g5.

00:45:54.250 --> 00:45:56.470
Here, white can even take on g5, but

00:45:56.470 --> 00:45:59.170
Mikhalevsky was planning f5, and black

00:45:59.170 --> 00:46:00.590
literally can't untangle.

00:46:00.710 --> 00:46:03.590
g6, f6, and the king is stuck on the

00:46:03.590 --> 00:46:04.750
eighth rank, which means the knight is

00:46:04.750 --> 00:46:05.730
stuck on the eighth rank, which means

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:07.070
the bishop is stuck on the eighth rank,

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:08.830
and white can win this pretty easily.

00:46:09.170 --> 00:46:10.450
Here's how the game actually ended.

00:46:10.570 --> 00:46:13.570
Black starts exhausting his moves, and

00:46:13.570 --> 00:46:14.590
here's what Mikhalevsky does.

00:46:14.770 --> 00:46:17.890
He literally waits around until black

00:46:17.890 --> 00:46:19.790
has exhausted all of his pawn moves,

00:46:20.030 --> 00:46:21.090
and look at this position.

00:46:21.490 --> 00:46:22.530
Black is in zugzwang.

00:46:22.630 --> 00:46:25.090
Black has no moves to avoid losing a

00:46:25.090 --> 00:46:27.370
minor piece because either you move the

00:46:27.370 --> 00:46:28.510
knight, which is what happened in the

00:46:28.510 --> 00:46:30.650
game, and black lost, or you have to

00:46:30.650 --> 00:46:32.670
move the king, and you lose to a fork.

00:46:32.910 --> 00:46:35.090
If you move the pawn, well, black could

00:46:35.090 --> 00:46:37.870
even, white could even take, but f5 is

00:46:37.870 --> 00:46:39.270
the elegant move because it really

00:46:39.270 --> 00:46:41.750
highlights the extent of white's domination.

00:46:42.570 --> 00:46:44.590
So the move was g6, and this is why

00:46:44.590 --> 00:46:46.310
piece activity always determines the

00:46:46.310 --> 00:46:48.870
evaluation in rook versus minor piece situations.

00:46:49.510 --> 00:46:52.350
Here's the second game that I had in

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:54.230
mind, and this is queen against two

00:46:54.230 --> 00:46:55.570
minor pieces, which is what we got at

00:46:55.570 --> 00:46:58.030
the very end of our game, but I had a

00:46:58.030 --> 00:47:01.090
game many years ago that was a lot less

00:47:01.090 --> 00:47:04.430
clear -cut than the speedrun game we

00:47:04.430 --> 00:47:05.530
played, which was just dead winning.

00:47:06.210 --> 00:47:08.770
This is a very old game of mine.

00:47:09.230 --> 00:47:11.590
As you can see from 2006, that was

00:47:11.590 --> 00:47:15.070
1900, and we reached this very unique endgame.

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:17.490
Queen, two minor pieces, and two pawns

00:47:17.490 --> 00:47:17.950
against queen.

00:47:18.630 --> 00:47:20.290
This is a very hard endgame to evaluate.

00:47:20.390 --> 00:47:22.090
Is this a draw or is this a win for black?

00:47:22.810 --> 00:47:24.710
Why are we even discussing this?

00:47:24.750 --> 00:47:26.630
White basically has a fortress, right?

00:47:27.090 --> 00:47:29.550
White's both pawns are defended by the

00:47:29.550 --> 00:47:31.530
bishop and the king respectively, and

00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:33.930
black's pawns are gap -toothed, which

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:36.610
means black can't use their pawns to

00:47:36.610 --> 00:47:39.230
dislodge white's minor pieces.

00:47:39.330 --> 00:47:40.450
You need to find some other way.

00:47:41.090 --> 00:47:42.490
Let's flip it so we're facing black.

00:47:43.570 --> 00:47:45.670
What I started to do earlier in the

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:47.650
game is I brought my king to white's

00:47:47.650 --> 00:47:49.390
side of the board, and I didn't know

00:47:49.390 --> 00:47:50.810
exactly how that was going to help, but

00:47:50.810 --> 00:47:52.390
I thought that might be a good start.

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:54.390
For a while, I was unable to make any

00:47:54.390 --> 00:47:55.950
progress, and I was unable to make

00:47:55.950 --> 00:47:58.430
progress for so long, and this is a

00:47:58.430 --> 00:48:00.910
draw that I ultimately gave up hope.

00:48:00.950 --> 00:48:03.750
I was hoping to deliver some made, but

00:48:03.750 --> 00:48:06.030
my opponent just correctly shuffles the

00:48:06.030 --> 00:48:10.290
pieces, but then at some point, we

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:11.270
repeat and repeat and repeat.

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:13.870
At some point here, my opponent makes a

00:48:13.870 --> 00:48:14.810
very sloppy move.

00:48:15.330 --> 00:48:16.930
He plays king f3.

00:48:17.050 --> 00:48:19.170
He should have played king to f1, so he

00:48:19.170 --> 00:48:20.370
lets my queen into e1.

00:48:20.530 --> 00:48:22.030
The king hides away on g2.

00:48:22.210 --> 00:48:26.010
Okay, then I played king to d2, and now

00:48:26.010 --> 00:48:27.010
things are getting serious.

00:48:27.150 --> 00:48:29.850
It's still a draw, but white can play

00:48:29.850 --> 00:48:32.330
bishop f5, make a draw, but my opponent

00:48:32.330 --> 00:48:33.470
was worried about king e2.

00:48:33.590 --> 00:48:34.950
He forgot that the bishop can just come

00:48:34.950 --> 00:48:38.670
back to g4, and this is a dead draw.

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:41.890
Black literally cannot get to the f2 pawn.

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:44.290
Hopefully, that makes sense, but he

00:48:44.290 --> 00:48:47.270
plays bishop g4 to d1 and offers a

00:48:47.270 --> 00:48:49.130
draw, and I suddenly got scared.

00:48:49.270 --> 00:48:50.490
I was like, wait, the queen is trapped,

00:48:50.730 --> 00:48:51.650
and I took a draw.

00:48:52.030 --> 00:48:53.830
Bishop d1 is a losing move.

00:48:53.890 --> 00:48:55.910
Who can tell me why, and I analyzed

00:48:55.910 --> 00:48:57.290
this for a long time with my coach.

00:48:57.610 --> 00:48:59.090
We came up with some very cool lines here.

00:48:59.190 --> 00:49:00.610
As far as we know, this is analysis

00:49:00.610 --> 00:49:01.450
from 2005.

00:49:01.670 --> 00:49:02.230
This is a loss.

00:49:02.770 --> 00:49:05.490
No, the mot point is queen takes d1.

00:49:05.770 --> 00:49:07.210
Remember from the pawn endgame videos,

00:49:07.210 --> 00:49:09.570
it's not about whose king is more advanced.

00:49:09.630 --> 00:49:11.990
It's about whose king is closer to the pawns.

00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:13.450
If you think of it that way, you should

00:49:13.450 --> 00:49:15.490
understand that white's king is very

00:49:15.490 --> 00:49:17.070
close to the h4 pawn.

00:49:17.150 --> 00:49:18.030
This is not going to be winning.

00:49:18.310 --> 00:49:19.850
This is probably a draw, but I think

00:49:19.850 --> 00:49:21.310
even black is close to losing this

00:49:21.310 --> 00:49:23.770
because this type of, okay, it's a draw.

00:49:23.810 --> 00:49:26.710
It's a draw, but black draws by like

00:49:26.710 --> 00:49:27.750
narrow thread.

00:49:28.170 --> 00:49:30.530
It's not queen takes d1, and it's not

00:49:30.530 --> 00:49:33.190
queen takes d3, similar reasoning.

00:49:33.750 --> 00:49:35.050
Black -white is going to be the first to

00:49:35.050 --> 00:49:37.930
take the pawn, so this is not the right approach.

00:49:38.070 --> 00:49:39.370
Instead, you have to notice that the

00:49:39.370 --> 00:49:41.290
bishop on d1 is very vulnerable because

00:49:41.290 --> 00:49:42.390
it's protected by the knight.

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:43.570
The move is f5.

00:49:43.650 --> 00:49:44.150
Yeah, f5.

00:49:44.810 --> 00:49:46.010
How does that f5 help?

00:49:46.090 --> 00:49:47.290
Because the bishop, of course, can go

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:48.350
back, let's say, to f3.

00:49:49.070 --> 00:49:50.810
Now, as far as I remember, you're

00:49:50.810 --> 00:49:52.210
supposed to play f4.

00:49:53.270 --> 00:49:54.470
You're supposed to play f4.

00:49:55.230 --> 00:49:56.570
White has basically two options.

00:49:56.650 --> 00:49:57.910
Where is white going to go with his knight?

00:49:57.990 --> 00:49:59.330
Well, probably white is going to go to

00:49:59.330 --> 00:50:02.010
g4 because if white goes elsewhere, the

00:50:02.010 --> 00:50:03.750
pawn on f2 can be surrounded.

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:07.410
You can go like this, and then white is

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:09.670
almost in zugzwang because white has no moves.

00:50:09.750 --> 00:50:10.890
White has to go back with his king.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:14.210
You can deliver a check on a1, and

00:50:14.210 --> 00:50:16.250
basically the bottom line idea is you

00:50:16.250 --> 00:50:18.770
like go here, and you're trying to go

00:50:18.770 --> 00:50:19.370
king e1.

00:50:20.210 --> 00:50:22.130
You're trying to set up this situation

00:50:22.130 --> 00:50:24.390
where the pawn cannot be defended.

00:50:25.130 --> 00:50:27.230
White can only stop you in one way to

00:50:27.230 --> 00:50:29.910
go king f1, and who can find the most

00:50:29.910 --> 00:50:31.090
accurate move in this position?

00:50:31.190 --> 00:50:32.830
Let's see who understands how this

00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:33.470
endgame works.

00:50:33.610 --> 00:50:34.590
Black has a winning move here.

00:50:35.370 --> 00:50:36.790
How do you clear the way for the king?

00:50:38.920 --> 00:50:40.080
There's only one correct check.

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:41.260
Peter Perturb got it.

00:50:41.560 --> 00:50:43.980
It's queen to c4 check because you have

00:50:43.980 --> 00:50:45.620
to keep the f4 pawn protected.

00:50:46.040 --> 00:50:47.460
Then you drive your king into e1.

00:50:47.600 --> 00:50:48.360
White is in zugzwang.

00:50:48.700 --> 00:50:51.140
White has no pieces to move other than

00:50:51.140 --> 00:50:53.380
his king, and now queen f1 is just game over.

00:50:54.640 --> 00:50:56.680
Okay, so this is winning.

00:50:56.980 --> 00:50:59.480
That means that white has to sit on g4

00:50:59.480 --> 00:51:01.500
because on g4, the knight is

00:51:01.500 --> 00:51:03.240
unassailable, and it's defending f2,

00:51:03.340 --> 00:51:04.680
but I'm pretty sure the black is still

00:51:04.680 --> 00:51:05.120
winning here.

00:51:06.060 --> 00:51:07.520
How is black winning here?

00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:09.720
Let me try to remember.

00:51:11.320 --> 00:51:12.220
Or is he winning?

00:51:12.300 --> 00:51:17.440
Yes, black is winning because I think

00:51:17.440 --> 00:51:19.020
what you're supposed to do here, you

00:51:19.020 --> 00:51:20.760
start with a move like queen a1, okay?

00:51:21.860 --> 00:51:24.200
So white has to make some random bishop move.

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:27.940
You get in with your king, but then you

00:51:27.940 --> 00:51:29.440
make this very important move.

00:51:30.060 --> 00:51:32.660
Here you play like queen to a6, I think.

00:51:32.760 --> 00:51:34.000
I don't remember how this wins.

00:51:34.100 --> 00:51:34.880
Maybe this is a draw.

00:51:35.180 --> 00:51:36.020
Maybe we were wrong.

00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:37.280
Let me check the engine.

00:51:38.160 --> 00:51:39.120
Let me check the engine here.

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:39.820
This is interesting.

00:51:39.940 --> 00:51:41.680
f5, but by the way, it shows you how

00:51:41.680 --> 00:51:43.900
resilient minor pieces are at holding

00:51:43.900 --> 00:51:44.480
things together.

00:51:44.600 --> 00:51:46.580
It's like very, very hard to crack these.

00:51:46.700 --> 00:51:47.300
Yeah, it is winning.

00:51:47.360 --> 00:51:47.760
It is winning.

00:51:48.580 --> 00:51:49.160
Bishop f3.

00:51:49.280 --> 00:51:50.180
Oh, f4 is wrong.

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:50.880
I apologize.

00:51:51.060 --> 00:51:51.940
f4 is not the move.

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:56.420
The move is queen to b1, and basically,

00:51:56.600 --> 00:51:56.960
white loses.

00:51:57.100 --> 00:51:58.420
So white makes a random bishop move,

00:51:58.920 --> 00:52:03.820
and now you play king to e1, and if

00:52:03.820 --> 00:52:05.420
white makes a random bishop move, oh,

00:52:05.480 --> 00:52:06.720
you just pick up the pawn on f2.

00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:07.940
You don't need to play f4.

00:52:08.320 --> 00:52:08.920
What am I saying?

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:11.220
Of course, f4 is useless because

00:52:11.220 --> 00:52:12.940
ultimately, you're going to push white

00:52:12.940 --> 00:52:13.440
off the board.

00:52:13.540 --> 00:52:17.500
Check here, here, and that's it.

00:52:17.580 --> 00:52:20.120
You're threatening checkmate on g1, so

00:52:20.120 --> 00:52:21.360
white has to move the knight and give

00:52:21.360 --> 00:52:23.300
up the pawn on f2, and there's just

00:52:23.300 --> 00:52:24.500
nothing that white can do.

00:52:24.540 --> 00:52:25.120
You just get in.

00:52:25.160 --> 00:52:26.540
You get into e1 with the king.

00:52:27.140 --> 00:52:28.600
White has just no other moves.

00:52:28.620 --> 00:52:29.920
If white gives the check, then you get

00:52:29.920 --> 00:52:33.380
in anyway, and now you go queen b2.

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:34.460
You attack the pawn.

00:52:34.580 --> 00:52:36.260
White's knight is no checks, like the

00:52:36.260 --> 00:52:38.400
king can't be assailed, and then you do

00:52:38.400 --> 00:52:39.680
this thing where you go king to queen

00:52:39.680 --> 00:52:41.680
g7, and then king f1 to push the king away.

00:52:42.160 --> 00:52:45.360
It's a cool idea, but I didn't see any

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:47.060
of that, and you couldn't do that earlier.

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:51.420
Like here, if white waits around with

00:52:51.420 --> 00:52:53.840
any move other than bishop f1, it's

00:52:53.840 --> 00:52:55.920
harder to get the king in because,

00:52:56.080 --> 00:52:57.220
okay, you can go queen c1.

00:52:58.020 --> 00:53:01.700
You can go here, but there is, I think,

00:53:01.960 --> 00:53:03.600
ah, the point is white can go like king

00:53:03.600 --> 00:53:05.500
g1, and then at the right moment,

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:06.120
knight g2 check.

00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:07.900
So somehow here, white holds, but with

00:53:07.900 --> 00:53:09.960
a pawn on f5, black takes away key

00:53:09.960 --> 00:53:12.060
squares from the bishop and the knight.

00:53:12.920 --> 00:53:14.940
Okay, let's move on to the game.

00:53:15.280 --> 00:53:17.720
Hopefully, this was semi -illuminating.

00:53:18.980 --> 00:53:20.820
It just came to my mind because I

00:53:20.820 --> 00:53:21.900
remember the two minor pieces.

00:53:22.980 --> 00:53:26.960
So in the game, once we played queen

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:28.880
b7, I could sense that we were close to winning.

00:53:29.120 --> 00:53:31.740
Then we initiated the forced queen

00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:33.980
trade, and knight g6 is another mistake.

00:53:34.200 --> 00:53:35.880
This is a very, very bad move because

00:53:35.880 --> 00:53:36.740
now the knight is passive.

00:53:36.860 --> 00:53:38.040
Black has to go knight c4.

00:53:38.140 --> 00:53:40.100
There's no question that the knight has

00:53:40.100 --> 00:53:41.580
to keep contact with the b6 square.

00:53:41.920 --> 00:53:43.860
What I was talking about is this would

00:53:43.860 --> 00:53:45.960
be very careless because of bishop b7,

00:53:46.000 --> 00:53:46.980
and black wins an exchange.

00:53:47.780 --> 00:53:49.200
Now, I want to point something out,

00:53:49.740 --> 00:53:51.540
which is that if white's pawn was on

00:53:51.540 --> 00:53:53.720
e4, if you had this position, this

00:53:53.720 --> 00:53:54.740
would be fine for white.

00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:56.880
So don't forget that if you have rook

00:53:56.880 --> 00:53:59.300
versus two minor pieces, you can gain

00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:01.000
the minor pieces again for the rook.

00:54:01.040 --> 00:54:02.600
So don't rule out lines where you lose

00:54:02.600 --> 00:54:04.940
your rook if you win both minor pieces.

00:54:05.380 --> 00:54:06.740
Of course, here we don't have the pawn

00:54:06.740 --> 00:54:08.280
on e4, so that doesn't work.

00:54:08.900 --> 00:54:12.060
So after knight g6, rook d8, okay, the

00:54:12.060 --> 00:54:12.600
game is over.

00:54:13.160 --> 00:54:15.520
But now, of course, I made a horrible mistake.

00:54:15.580 --> 00:54:17.060
f3 is correct, knight e5 is correct.

00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:19.640
Yeah, rook d4 is a big mistake.

00:54:19.760 --> 00:54:21.940
It's still winning after bishop f3,

00:54:22.020 --> 00:54:22.380
still winning.

00:54:23.060 --> 00:54:26.080
But the best move was just to take the

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:28.380
rook and go rook c7, and then promote

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:28.720
the pawn.

00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:30.220
I don't understand why I didn't play

00:54:30.220 --> 00:54:32.520
this, but this ends the game immediately.

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:35.640
Yeah, rook f8 and rook c7, I don't know.

00:54:35.800 --> 00:54:39.340
I was somehow fixated on forcing black

00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:41.220
to be the one to take because I was

00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:42.760
obsessed with the idea of forcing the

00:54:42.760 --> 00:54:43.660
king in the other direction.

00:54:44.120 --> 00:54:46.860
Somehow, I didn't, like, realize that

00:54:46.860 --> 00:54:49.020
the king is still too far away from the pawn.

00:54:49.580 --> 00:54:51.360
But rook d4 would win if it weren't for

00:54:51.360 --> 00:54:51.960
bishop f3.

00:54:52.380 --> 00:54:53.940
Apparently, the winning move here, the

00:54:53.940 --> 00:54:55.820
only winning move, and let me explain

00:54:55.820 --> 00:54:57.160
why the pawn endgame is drawn.

00:54:57.520 --> 00:54:59.340
The reason the pawn endgame is drawn,

00:54:59.780 --> 00:55:01.220
first of all, is because the king

00:55:01.220 --> 00:55:02.260
catches the b -pawn.

00:55:02.860 --> 00:55:04.260
So you have the gap -toothed pawn

00:55:04.260 --> 00:55:05.960
formation, which is self -sufficient.

00:55:06.080 --> 00:55:07.660
The king can't take either of the pawns.

00:55:07.840 --> 00:55:08.580
That's fine.

00:55:09.200 --> 00:55:10.880
So you get this situation, black's king

00:55:10.880 --> 00:55:13.080
has to shuffle between d7 and d6.

00:55:13.620 --> 00:55:15.060
The problem is that black is a three -on

00:55:15.060 --> 00:55:16.780
-one on the queen side, and white cannot

00:55:16.780 --> 00:55:19.200
defeat a three -on -one with his king alone.

00:55:19.920 --> 00:55:21.580
Like, black will play here, and then

00:55:21.580 --> 00:55:24.060
here, and you have no way, even like

00:55:24.060 --> 00:55:25.500
f4, you have no way to make progress

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:27.160
because this is a protected passed pawn.

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:28.380
Does that make sense?

00:55:28.440 --> 00:55:30.700
So this is just a dead -drawn pawn endgame.

00:55:31.220 --> 00:55:33.640
The move, apparently, is e4.

00:55:33.820 --> 00:55:35.280
You have to cut off the bishop.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:37.360
You have to cut off the bishop, and on

00:55:37.360 --> 00:55:38.480
the next move, you play b6.

00:55:38.600 --> 00:55:39.220
Bishop e2?

00:55:39.820 --> 00:55:40.380
Actually, no.

00:55:40.460 --> 00:55:42.060
Apparently, you throw in rook d5 first

00:55:42.060 --> 00:55:42.680
to hit the knight.

00:55:43.220 --> 00:55:44.420
Now you play b6.

00:55:45.340 --> 00:55:46.920
Okay, so you're threatening b7.

00:55:47.040 --> 00:55:48.320
Okay, so black has to stop the pawn.

00:55:48.640 --> 00:55:49.860
Now you deliver a check.

00:55:50.080 --> 00:55:51.960
You go around to a8.

00:55:52.220 --> 00:55:54.280
Oh, wow, and black can't go here

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:55.280
because of rook a7.

00:55:55.420 --> 00:55:55.720
Beautiful.

00:55:56.480 --> 00:55:58.360
So you're forcing the bishop away, and

00:55:58.360 --> 00:55:59.360
now you promote to a pawn.

00:55:59.440 --> 00:56:00.960
You force black to give up his knight.

00:56:01.720 --> 00:56:04.980
By the way, I see this mistake so often.

00:56:05.420 --> 00:56:07.220
If black plays knight c6 here, who can

00:56:07.220 --> 00:56:09.560
tell me what the best move is for white?

00:56:09.880 --> 00:56:11.020
Is it to promote the pawn?

00:56:11.480 --> 00:56:14.280
This is a mistake I see very, very often.

00:56:15.840 --> 00:56:16.680
Rook c8, yeah.

00:56:17.060 --> 00:56:18.700
Rook c8 wins on the spot because you

00:56:18.700 --> 00:56:19.860
actually give up the rook for the

00:56:19.860 --> 00:56:21.280
knight, and you promote to a queen.

00:56:21.600 --> 00:56:24.000
So don't automatically promote when

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:25.480
your opponent is stopping the minor

00:56:25.480 --> 00:56:27.180
pawn, the pawn to the minor piece.

00:56:27.320 --> 00:56:29.320
See if you can essentially capture the

00:56:29.320 --> 00:56:30.520
minor piece because then you can

00:56:30.520 --> 00:56:31.400
promote the entire pawn.

00:56:32.260 --> 00:56:34.500
After knight d7, black, white doesn't

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:36.760
have the same option because the knight

00:56:36.760 --> 00:56:38.420
can't be a sale, but this is winning.

00:56:38.820 --> 00:56:40.420
This is theoretically winning endgame,

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:42.300
but some work would still be required

00:56:42.300 --> 00:56:43.940
for us to win this, and definitely

00:56:43.940 --> 00:56:45.200
there was not need to blunder this.

00:56:45.600 --> 00:56:47.280
These can be very, very hard to win.

00:56:47.380 --> 00:56:50.420
Like 3 -on -3 can be very, very, very

00:56:50.420 --> 00:56:50.920
hard to win.

00:56:51.780 --> 00:56:55.340
So definitely this should not have been allowed.

00:56:55.920 --> 00:56:57.320
You want to hear something crazy?

00:56:57.840 --> 00:56:59.820
So do you remember I said you throw in

00:56:59.820 --> 00:57:00.320
this move?

00:57:00.640 --> 00:57:02.160
Why not b6 immediately?

00:57:02.300 --> 00:57:02.980
Here's the difference.

00:57:03.060 --> 00:57:05.060
So here you've got the same line, rook a8.

00:57:05.580 --> 00:57:07.340
Black plays bishop d, sorry, black

00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:10.980
plays, no, here apparently black plays

00:57:10.980 --> 00:57:13.220
some sort of weird king e7.

00:57:13.840 --> 00:57:18.680
Oh, and if you start with rook d5, in

00:57:18.680 --> 00:57:21.260
this position, there is no king e7

00:57:21.260 --> 00:57:23.120
because here you can take the knight

00:57:23.120 --> 00:57:24.360
and play b7.

00:57:24.660 --> 00:57:27.320
So you're forcing bishop a6, whereas

00:57:27.320 --> 00:57:29.000
there, there's some tricky king e7 move.

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:31.580
So many of these subtleties, these

00:57:31.580 --> 00:57:32.880
types of positions, amazing.

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:34.520
Just as honestly amazing.

00:57:34.780 --> 00:57:36.040
Okay, after our opponent played bishop

00:57:36.040 --> 00:57:37.860
b7, the only critical move was f4,

00:57:38.200 --> 00:57:39.320
forcing the knight away.

00:57:39.440 --> 00:57:41.600
Look at the domination, c6 controlled,

00:57:41.740 --> 00:57:43.700
d7 controlled, c4 controlled.

00:57:44.060 --> 00:57:45.320
The knight has to drop all the way back

00:57:45.320 --> 00:57:47.960
and bang, rook d7, again, domination.

00:57:48.620 --> 00:57:50.140
Bishop can't go back to the 8th, and we

00:57:50.140 --> 00:57:52.700
go, we'll go b6, and b7 is going to decide.

00:57:53.020 --> 00:57:54.740
Knight e7, rook d8 is checkmate, by the way.

00:57:54.800 --> 00:57:56.380
Don't forget this mating pattern.

00:57:56.700 --> 00:57:58.100
Even with a king on f8, it's still

00:57:58.100 --> 00:57:59.280
possible to back rank mate.

00:58:00.820 --> 00:58:02.440
King e8, b7, and that's it.

00:58:02.540 --> 00:58:03.420
b8 queen is happening.

00:58:03.620 --> 00:58:04.840
Of course, this is technically

00:58:05.040 --> 00:58:07.280
nominally more resilient, but four on

00:58:07.280 --> 00:58:09.540
three plus an extra exchange is easy win.

00:58:09.720 --> 00:58:11.480
You just get your king up, and then you

00:58:11.480 --> 00:58:13.320
basically just push your pawns to oblivion.

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:15.480
This is super, super straightforward

00:58:15.480 --> 00:58:16.640
winning process here.

00:58:17.780 --> 00:58:20.660
Okay, so takeaways from this really

00:58:20.660 --> 00:58:21.400
excellent game.

00:58:21.480 --> 00:58:22.680
First of all, a huge kudos to our

00:58:22.680 --> 00:58:24.460
opponent for finding this queen e8,

00:58:24.460 --> 00:58:25.140
knight e4 idea.

00:58:25.600 --> 00:58:27.200
So the opening was a jabava line, and I

00:58:27.200 --> 00:58:28.360
want to remind people I have a course

00:58:28.360 --> 00:58:29.960
on this opening that will be linked in

00:58:29.960 --> 00:58:32.260
the description that I released

00:58:32.260 --> 00:58:34.180
together with GM Alexander Bortnik.

00:58:34.660 --> 00:58:36.020
So this follows the course.

00:58:36.200 --> 00:58:37.380
This all follows the course.

00:58:37.780 --> 00:58:40.960
My biggest mistake was not to ask what

00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:42.820
my opponent wanted with this move.

00:58:42.860 --> 00:58:44.060
In fact, I asked what he wanted.

00:58:44.360 --> 00:58:45.620
I thought he wanted rook d8.

00:58:45.920 --> 00:58:47.260
So the correct move here is to

00:58:47.260 --> 00:58:48.920
preemptively move the knight away from

00:58:48.920 --> 00:58:51.220
an undefended square and to take the

00:58:51.220 --> 00:58:52.500
sting out of the move, knight e4.

00:58:52.700 --> 00:58:54.620
That would have given white a clearly

00:58:54.620 --> 00:58:55.280
better position.

00:58:55.920 --> 00:58:57.640
Instead, once we blundered the two

00:58:57.640 --> 00:58:59.460
pieces for a rook, black was better,

00:58:59.620 --> 00:59:01.700
but we did our best to activate our pieces.

00:59:02.180 --> 00:59:04.520
We found resilient moves all the way through.

00:59:05.060 --> 00:59:06.640
And the critical mistake for black,

00:59:07.040 --> 00:59:08.080
black made a couple of mistakes.

00:59:08.200 --> 00:59:10.400
First of all, rook f8 was a waste of time.

00:59:10.480 --> 00:59:11.960
The immediate queen f6 would have been

00:59:11.960 --> 00:59:13.580
very problematic for us.

00:59:13.920 --> 00:59:16.020
This allowed us to make loft, and then

00:59:16.020 --> 00:59:17.720
the main mistake that black played is

00:59:17.720 --> 00:59:18.700
to take the pawn on a3.

00:59:18.800 --> 00:59:19.960
Black should have taken the other pawn.

00:59:20.020 --> 00:59:21.800
When you have the minor pieces against

00:59:21.800 --> 00:59:23.340
a rook, your top priority should

00:59:23.340 --> 00:59:25.740
basically be to hold off your

00:59:25.740 --> 00:59:26.900
opponent's pawn mass.

00:59:27.040 --> 00:59:28.820
Because once the pawn mass starts

00:59:28.820 --> 00:59:31.440
rolling, rooks are really, really good

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:33.440
at supervising past pawns.

00:59:33.820 --> 00:59:35.880
So you want to keep things immobilized

00:59:35.880 --> 00:59:36.740
as much as possible.

00:59:37.220 --> 00:59:38.300
Any questions?

